<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#myrrh">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Myrrh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Myrrh is an aromatic resin obtained from trees native to north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, prized in antiquity for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, perfume and embalming. In the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> it was a key import from the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s expedition to Punt brought back myrrh trees along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and exotic animals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/myrrh.jpg" alt="Myrrh" width="190" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sjschen; derivative work by Photohound · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Commiphora-myrrha-resin-myrrh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Myrrh is a gum-resin from a few small, thorny species of the genus Commiphora, in the family Burseraceae. The tree secretes it when a cut penetrates the bark into the sapwood, and it is harvested by repeatedly cutting the trees. The gum is waxy and coagulates quickly, then hardens and turns glossy. It is yellowish, clear or opaque, and darkens as it ages. Commiphora myrrha is native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Somaliland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somali-region">Somali Region</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and parts of Saudi Arabia. The word comes from a Semitic root m-r-r, meaning bitter, and entered English through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, where it is mōr.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The earliest attested expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>, recorded by the 5th-dynasty king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahure">Sahure</a>, brought back large quantities of myrrh and frankincense along with malachite, electrum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and animals. A relief from his mortuary temple shows Sahure tending a myrrh tree in his palace garden, the only Egyptian relief that shows a king gardening. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> used myrrh with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natron">natron</a> to embalm mummies. In the first century BCE Diodorus Siculus recorded that Nabataean caravans and ports carried it from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, which controlled the Incense Route and taxed the trade.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> names myrrh as a rare perfume. Exodus 30:23 to 25 prescribes 500 shekels of liquid myrrh for the sacred anointing oil, it was an ingredient of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> temples, and Esther 2:12 gives six months of oil of myrrh to the women prepared for King Ahasuerus. In medicine it served as an antiseptic in mouthwashes and salves and as a painkiller for toothache and bruises, and mixed with wine it was used to dull pain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> lists myrrh among the three gifts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">magi</a>, with gold and frankincense, and says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was offered wine mixed with myrrh at his crucifixion and that Nicodemus and Joseph of Arimathea brought a mixture of myrrh and aloes for his burial. Myrrh is still burned as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> with frankincense in Eastern Orthodox, Oriental Orthodox, traditional Roman Catholic and Anglican churches, is added to egg tempera in icon painting, and is used to prepare the chrism for chrismation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a> advises fumigating houses with mugwort, myrrh and thyme.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Myrrh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state took a direct part in long-distance trade, as in Hatshepsut's Punt expedition, which brought back <b>myrrh</b> trees, ebony, ivory, and exotic animals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy also depended on long-distance trade: frankincense and <b>myrrh</b> from the south and east, and the transit of Indian and African goods through ports such as Muza and Aden, described in the first-century Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, which names Charibael (Karib'il) as king of the Homerites…</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Saba also lay on the caravan routes that carried frankincense and <b>myrrh</b>, gathered from trees in Hadramawt, Dhofar and the Horn of Africa, north through western Arabia to Gaza and the Mediterranean and to Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt's growing appetite for ebony, incense such as <b>myrrh</b> and frankincense, gold, copper and other useful metals drove the building of ships fit for the open sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings grew rich as the terminus of caravan routes from Arabia carrying frankincense and <b>myrrh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza">Gaza</a></li><li><blockquote>In 205 BCE the Seleucid king Antiochus III sailed to Gerrha and accepted a tribute of silver, frankincense and <b>myrrh</b> in return for leaving it free.</blockquote><a href="/state/gerrha">Gerrha</a></li></ul></section>
